00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Our first guests tonight
are the co-authors

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the number-one
"New York Times" best seller

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The President's Daughter,
which is out now.

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:06.606 line:92%
Please welcome to the show

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:10.510 size:81% position:41%
President Bill Clinton
and James Patterson.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.345 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Applause ]

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.114 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:16.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:20.053
Hello, James. How are you?
Good to see you. Come on up.

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:23.189 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.758 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Welcome, gentlemen.

00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:26.026 line:92%
Here we are, big-time authors.

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:28.895 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, cool. I get a couch now
-You get a whole couch.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:30.163 line:92%
-You might get to recline.

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:31.865
-We saw -- We were going t
have two chairs, but then,

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:35.869
we heard your writer insiste
that you have your own couch

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.137 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Can I lay out?

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:39.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Now, I want to start
by clarifying.

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:40.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is about --
Your previous book

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:42.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
was about a president
going missing.

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is about
a president's daughter

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:45.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
going missing,
different president.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.514 size:78% position:39%
-Different president.
-And out of office.

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:49.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And out of office.
-Just out of office.

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:52.519
-Where do you get your ideas
a president out of office?

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:53.753 line:92%
-Yeah, what a crazy idea.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.755 line:92%
-Well, this actually is a --

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.224 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think has
a very interesting premise

00:00:58.224 --> 00:01:00.827 size:81% position:41% line:92%
because what you find,

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:04.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you leave the White House
things change.

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:05.732 line:92%
I mean, they don't play a song

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:07.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you walk in the room
anymore.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And you lose -- You keep

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:12.005 line:92%
your Secret Service protection

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:16.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
for yourself and your spouse
but it's less.

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:19.412 align:start size:94% position:6%
And your children over 18
don't get it.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.281 size:81% position:41%
-Oh, I didn't realize
they lose it after 18.

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:25.718
-Yeah, and so this is a stor
about how the things you d

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:28.888 size:81% position:41%
when you're president
that have consequences

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:30.323 size:84% position:42% line:92%
other people don't like

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:33.259 align:start size:94% position:6%
can follow you
out of the office

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:36.463 line:92%
ad fall on your children.

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:38.731 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Obviously, in your books,
these are very --

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:41.968
You know, they're thrillers.
A lot of extreme things happen

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.938 size:88% position:44%
And yet we've seen since
your first book in 2018

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:49.109 align:start size:94% position:6%
even more extreme events
happen with real presidents.

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:49.976 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Has it been harder

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:52.879
to write things that make people
say, "Wow, that's amazing"

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:55.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, back in the '60s,
Philip Roth --

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:56.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because we had Nixon
and Vietnam.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:59.986
He said, "I can't compete.
I'll never write fiction again."

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:02.555 align:start size:94% position:6%
He then eventually
did write fiction again.

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:04.190 line:92%
But that is an issue right now

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.558 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But if you look
at what happened

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:09.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
with the latest
cybersecurity stuff,

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:11.431 line:92%
our book looks pretty realistic.

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:13.133 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And this is also

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:16.669
very important, because we -
You're living in this worl

00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:20.473
where these, you know,
people can be omnipresent

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
for all kinds of reasons
everywhere.

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:25.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
And what you do
when you're president

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:26.279 size:84% position:42% line:92%
follows you afterwards.

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:28.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
So then you have to
figure out --

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:29.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, this is made
more interesting

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:35.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
by the alienation of the
president from his successor

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:38.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the uncertainty
that a former president ha

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:42.228 align:start size:94% position:6%
that his child
is going to be a top priority.

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:46.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you get a lot of interestin
cross-currents here.

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:47.867 line:92%
-I have heard that you --

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:51.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think when
we picture presidents reading,

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:52.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
we picture them
reading briefings

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:54.240 line:92%
or maybe reading nonfiction.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.776 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you did find time in offic
to read fiction.

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:59.879 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was that something
that helped as an escape

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.281 line:92%
from the rigors of the job

00:03:01.281 --> 00:03:03.716 align:start size:94% position:6%
or did you actually
find inspiration

00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:06.052 size:88% position:44%
in the pages of fiction
when you were in office?

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:10.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, both.
I started in the early '80s.

00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:16.429
I started reading thrillers,
mysteries, detective novels.

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:18.398 line:92%
I was always fascinated by them.

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:21.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I found them --
the good ones -- really in

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know,
great character portraits.

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:26.306 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, a lot of the presidents
actually read

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:27.373 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
a lot of thrillers.

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:28.875 size:75% position:38%
The Bushes both did,
President Clinton.

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:31.711
I don't know about President
Trump, but, you know, whatever

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:35.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He lived it. It was a thrill.
It was a thrill ride.

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:40.653
-And I read -- On occasion
I would read fiction books

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:45.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I thought they might
be an omen of what to do.

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:47.260 line:92%
And I read "The Hot Zone,"

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
Richard Preston's
"The Hot Zone."

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:52.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it really accelerated
my efforts

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:57.370 size:88% position:44%
to get the White House
to set up a special unit

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:03.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
on dealing with biological
and chemical outbreaks.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:05.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
And especially
in the biological area,

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:08.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
both deliberate
biological warfare

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:10.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the possibility
of pandemics.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:15.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we started working on this
in about 1998.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:19.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's -- We hatched
a lot of plans out.

00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:22.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
We did a lot of
kind of war gaming,

00:04:22.362 --> 00:04:24.998 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean real efforts
with security people

00:04:24.998 --> 00:04:26.199 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
all over America.

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:27.934 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we set up
the first stockpile.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:34.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I've been waiting
for what happened with COVID

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:34.974 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and we were --

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:37.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
Look, with SARS, with MERS
with all this stuff,

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:39.445 size:75% position:38%
we were given plenty
of advanced warning

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
that something like this
could happen.

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:42.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Now James,
as an author who's aware

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:45.385 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you have presidents
in office who are reading book

00:04:45.385 --> 00:04:47.687 align:start size:94% position:6%
and oftentimes
reading your books,

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:50.556
do you have a sense of the power
you might have to influenc

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.358 align:start size:94% position:6%
based on what you put
in your pages?

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:54.694 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I have no power.
-[ Laughter ]

00:04:54.694 --> 00:04:57.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm here with Seth, I'm her
with President Clinton.

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:58.631 size:75% position:38% line:92%
I'm the third wheel,

00:04:58.631 --> 00:04:59.666 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that's the way it is
in life.

00:04:59.666 --> 00:05:01.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know how it is for writers
You're a writer.

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:02.902 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, I'm a writer
and oftentimes --

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:04.170 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-Nobody listens to us.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.239 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughter ]
-Nobody listens to him.

00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:08.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's how he sold
400 million books.

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:10.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I didn't say
they don't read me.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.445 line:92%
They just don't listen to me

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:16.616
-When -- Do you guys ever have
arguments about a plot point

00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:20.153
And then, is that a situation,
James, where a former presiden

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.822
maybe pulls rank on even someone
who's sold 400 million books

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:24.691
-We have never --
No, we've never had an argument.

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:25.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, we'll talk
about the different --

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:28.394
You know, one of the thing
I think that separates us --

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:31.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because a lot of the books
a lot of thrillers,

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.732 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
we make stuff up.

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:34.400 align:start size:94% position:6%
If I'm writing
with the president,

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
some of the stuff might be
a little over the top,

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:40.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
although nothing's
over the top these days.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:42.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the president makes sure
that if it happens,

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:45.545
this is how it would happen,
and he's very strict about that.

00:05:45.545 --> 00:05:49.082 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's I think
what separates these books

00:05:49.082 --> 00:05:49.882 line:92%
-We don't have arguments.

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:52.652 align:start size:94% position:6%
What we really have is we have
interesting discussions,

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:56.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
because now that
I have done this twice,

00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:58.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
basically,
as a student of a master,

00:05:58.825 --> 00:06:03.429
what I have learned is,
we start with an outline.

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:06.466 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have a beginning. We thin
we know what the end is.

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:07.533 size:84% position:42% line:92%
And we have an outline.

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:10.903
But then these people become
real as you write about them

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:13.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And the scenes become vivid

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:18.378
And so the story lines begin
to take a little different turn.

00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:21.280 size:88% position:44%
And so one of the things
we would have to do --

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.149 size:84% position:42%
Like in this last book,
we did it at the end --

00:06:23.149 --> 00:06:26.419 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have to say, "Hey, we'r
gonna have two loose ends here

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:29.122
Let's figure out
how we're going to tie them up."

00:06:29.122 --> 00:06:30.423 size:78% position:39% line:92%
And then, you know --

00:06:30.423 --> 00:06:32.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The endings
almost always change.

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:34.961
What I do -- and it's true
with the books we do together --

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:37.864
I like to pretend there's on
person sitting across from me,

00:06:37.864 --> 00:06:40.533 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I don't want them to get u
until we finish the story.

00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:42.468 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's kind of
what we try to do

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:44.937 line:92%
with "The President's Daughter."

00:06:44.937 --> 00:06:46.072 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-You --

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:48.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
I can only imagine
what it's like --

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:53.546
The way you write must be so
fast, James, based on output

00:06:53.546 --> 00:06:55.882 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are you someone
that responded to the process?

00:06:55.882 --> 00:06:57.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I know
it's every two years,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:59.652 size:81% position:41%
but you have certainly
written other books.

00:06:59.652 --> 00:07:01.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But it wasn't necessarily
every two years.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.689 size:84% position:42%
I don't know that we --
We missed each other.

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:05.725
That's why we got back together,
you know?

00:07:05.725 --> 00:07:07.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We had a good time.
-"Let's do another one." Yeah.

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:09.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hillary is writing a book
as well.

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:10.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We're not going to
talk about that.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.665 align:start size:94% position:6%
Let her come here,
and she can sell her own books

00:07:12.665 --> 00:07:16.436 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, I've heard
you're very competitive, yeah.

00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:17.870 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And she's doing it
with Louise Penny,

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:22.175
who is a fabulous mystery writer
and a good friend of ours.

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:23.309 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-She is.

00:07:23.309 --> 00:07:25.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So there's a little bit
of gender

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:28.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
and as well as friendship
competition here.

00:07:28.247 --> 00:07:30.883 size:88% position:44%
And I've read the book.
It's a really good book.

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:33.586 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's going to be released --
-Unfortunately.

00:07:33.586 --> 00:07:34.554 line:92%
No, it's very good, very good.

00:07:34.554 --> 00:07:37.557
-There's a lot of room
for everybody to succeed, James.

00:07:37.557 --> 00:07:38.491 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-No, there isn't.

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:41.127 size:88% position:44%
There's very little room
at the number-one spot.

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:42.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's number one,
and, you know...

00:07:42.862 --> 00:07:45.465 size:88% position:44%
-Well, I mean, you know,
the important thing is

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:46.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you get your name
out there.

00:07:46.866 --> 00:07:50.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think that's a very importan
thing for you to do.

